[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: false
faulty_devices: none
diagnosis_text:
DNS infrastructure is up. The SRV1 DNS daemon (named) is running and listening on 10.5.0.4:53 (srv1: “named -c /etc/bind/named.conf”; UDP 53 bound on 10.5.0.4). Clients are correctly pointed at SRV1 (pc1/pc3 /etc/resolv.conf: “nameserver 10.5.0.4”). Queries for campus names resolve: from pc3/pc1, nslookup srv1.jeremysitlab.com and other jeremysitlab.com hosts return A records (10.5.0.4), as defined by the authoritative zone file /etc/bind/db.jeremysitlab on SRV1. However, external names (e.g., google.com) are refused with “REFUSED.” This is because SRV1 is configured as an authoritative-only server with recursion disabled (srv1:/etc/bind/named.conf: “recursion no;”). That policy causes non-authoritative lookups to fail, which likely led users to believe “DNS is down.” Internal resolution is functioning; no crash or reachability issue was found.

Localization: SRV1 (10.5.0.4), BIND named process and configuration. Mechanism: BIND answers for the local zone, but refuses recursive queries, so external lookups fail while internal succeed.

Root cause: Recursion is intentionally disabled on SRV1, so clients cannot resolve non-jeremysitlab.com names and misinterpreted this as a DNS outage.

Corrective fix: If recursive resolution is desired, edit /etc/bind/named.conf to set “recursion yes;” and optionally configure “allow-recursion { your subnets; };” and forwarders/root hints, then reload named (rndc reload) or restart it. Otherwise, no restart is needed; DNS is healthy for the campus zone. === END ===